<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 76 0 R/T(Projecting the six tuples gives SC={\(S1,C1\),\(S1,C2\),\(S2,C1\),\(S2,C2\),\(S3,C1\),\(S3,C2\)}, CW={\(C1,W1\),\(C2,W2\),\(C1,W2\),\(C2,W1\)}, and SW={\(S1,W1\),\(S1,W2\),\(S2,W2\),\(S2,W1\),\(S3,W1\),\(S3,W2\)}. Joining these projections reconstructs the listed valid triples only if the cyclic rule is a true business constraint and the instance satisfies it. The chase checks whether the tuple symbols become identical in the joined tableau; in this case, the JD is the required constraint, not something inferable from ordinary FDs. )/E(Projecting the six tuples gives SC={\(S1,C1\),\(S1,C2\),\(S2,C1\),\(S2,C2\),\(S3,C1\),\(S3,C2\)}, CW={\(C1,W1\),\(C2,W2\),\(C1,W2\),\(C2,W1\)}, and SW={\(S1,W1\),\(S1,W2\),\(S2,W2\),\(S2,W1\),\(S3,W1\),\(S3,W2\)}. Joining these projections reconstructs the listed valid triples only if the cyclic rule is a true business constraint and the instance satisfies it. The chase checks whether the tuple symbols become identical in the joined tableau; in this case, the JD is the required constraint, not something inferable from ordinary FDs. )/Pg 20 0 R/K[31]>>
endobj

